  "text": "We present a quantum‑mechanical framework built in an abstract multi‑particle setting that models interactions occurring through the creation of a temporary combined entity. The discussion is limited to a simple system that mimics a single‑level scattering event. The system’s state is described in two parts: the incoming particles and the intermediate composite. Its evolution is governed by an energy‑like operator, derived from the conditions imposed at the system’s boundaries, which has no direct classical counterpart. We examine how the variables linked to each part are represented and list all conserved quantities of the model. Expanding any general state in terms of the simultaneous eigenstates of these conserved quantities yields a set of integral transforms previously used to obtain time‑dependent solutions for scattering and breakup. In the basis where the conserved quantities are diagonal, the operator for the relative motion of the incoming particles becomes off‑diagonal near the resonance region. A unitary matrix that maps an initial state to its form at any later time is derived, allowing the calculation of the time evolution of the observable quantities of the system."
